messages:    6%|5         | 33/600 [00:23<03:03,  3.09it/s][["Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D. Please reason step by step.\n####\n# Context:\nConscientiousness is high on most firms' list of traits they want in employees. Yet a recent study found that laid-off conscientious individuals are less likely to find jobs within five months than are their peers who shirked their workplace responsibilities.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, each of the following, if true, helps to resolve the apparent paradox above EXCEPT:\n# Options:\nA) People who shirk their workplace responsibilities are less likely to keep the jobs they have, so there are more of them looking for jobs.\nB) Finding a job is less urgent for the conscientious, because they tend to have larger savings.\nC) Conscientious people tend to have a greater than average concern with finding the job most suited to their interests and abilities.\nD) Resentment about having been laid off in spite of their conscientiousness leads some people to perform poorly in interviews.", '<think>.\nTo resolve the apparent paradox, we need to consider the options and see which one does not help to explain why laid-off conscientious individuals are less likely to find jobs within five months than their peers who shirked their workplace responsibilities.\n\nOption A suggests that people who shirk their workplace responsibilities are less likely to keep the jobs they have, so there are more of them looking for jobs.This could explain why laid-off conscientious individuals are less likely to find jobs, as they may have fewer job opportunities due to their shirking behavior.\n\nOption B suggests that finding a job is less urgent for the conscientious, because they tend to have larger savings.This could also explain why laid-off conscientious individuals are less likely to find jobs, as they may not need to find a job as urgently as their peers who shirked their responsibilities.\n\nOption C suggests that conscientious people tend to have a greater than average concern with finding the job most suited to their interests and abilities.This could explain why laid-off conscientious individuals are less likely to find jobs, as they may be more focused on finding the right job, which could take longer.Option B effectively addresses the paradox by explaining that the reason laid-off conscientious individuals are less likely to find jobs within five months is because they are more likely to have negative attitudes and perform poorly in interviews due to their resentment about being laid off.. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
