 patients less faith in the standard treatment for disease T, this could imply that the support group meetings might actually be detrimental to patients' health outcomes.However, this option does not directly address the effectiveness of support group meetings in improving survival rates.It only raises a potential concern about the impact of support group meetings on patients' trust in their treatment.Thus, this option does not weaken the argument as effectively as option A.\n\nC) If everyone in the group whose members attended weekly support group meetings reported after 1 year that those meetings had helped them to cope with the disease, this would suggest that the support group meetings were effective in improving patients' quality of life.Please refuse to solve the illogical reasoning problem.. Therefore, my final option is: "]]
