urers to produce more durable appliances.While this might be true, it does not directly weaken the argument about the effectiveness of the fee in reducing waste.It only addresses the potential impact on manufacturers' behavior.\n\nC) Increasing the cost of disposing of an appliance properly increases the incentive to dispose of it improperly.\nThis option suggests that increasing the cost of proper disposal might lead to improper disposal.However, this does not directly weaken the argument about the effectiveness of the fee in reducing waste.The option implies that the fee is immediately applicable to individuals who recently acquired new appliances, contrary to the suggestion in the argument. This directly strengthens the effectiveness of the fee in reducing waste by ensuring prompt payment from new appliance buyers.\n\nIn conclusion, option A does not significantly weaken the argument, but rather reinforces the importance of the fee in waste reduction.. Therefore, my final option is: "]]
