messages:   88%|########7 | 525/600 [18:32<00:13,  5.76it/s][['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D. Please reason step by step.\n####\n# Context:\nWittgenstein was a student of Moore, a famous philosopher at Cambridge University. One day, philosopher Russell asked Moore, who is your best student? Moore said without hesitation: Wittgenstein. why? because among all the students, only he always showed a blank look when listening to the class, and there were always endless questions. later, Wittgenstein is more famous than Russell. Someone asked, why did Russell fall behind? Wittgenstein said: he has no problem.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, this paragraph is intended to illustrate that:\n# Options:\nA) Doubting and questioning are always the secret of success\nB) Curiosity is the internal driving force of research\nC) Appreciation is an important factor in success\nD) Finding problems is the premise of continuous progress', "<think>.\nTo determine the correct answer, let's analyze the given context and question step by step:.\n\n1.The context mentions that Wittgenstein was a student of Moore, who was a famous philosopher at Cambridge University.\n2.Russell asked Moore who his best student was, and Moore immediately replied that it was Wittgenstein.\n3.The reason given by Moore was that Wittgenstein always showed a blank look when listening to the class and had endless questions.\n4.Later, Wittgenstein became more famous than Russell.\n5.Someone asked Wittgenstein why he fell behind, and Wittgenstein attributed it to the fact that he had no problem.\n\nNow, let's evaluate each option based on the context:.\n\nA) Doubting and questioning are always the secret of success: This option seems plausible, as Wittgenstein's endless questions and blank look when listening to the class suggest that he was constantly questioning and seeking answers.\n\nBelief and acceptance are the external hindrances of research: This option also seems implausible, as Wittgenstein's unquestioning acceptance and lack of curiosity about the subject matter could be seen as a hindrance for his research.\n\nB) Criticism is an insignificant factor in success: This option is more likely, as the context does not mention any criticism or disapproval of Wittgenstein's work.\n\nC) Avoiding problems is the foundation of continuous progress: This option is more likely, as the context does not mention any specific problems that Wittgenstein faced or solved.\n\nBased on the analysis, the least plausible answer is:.\n\nD) Trusting and affirming are never the key to success.\n\n<answer>\n. Therefore, my final option is: "]]
