ity between the skeletal fractures of prehistoric hominids and those of rodeo riders.\n\nC) If direct evidence as to the cause of a phenomenon is available, then indirect evidence should not be sought.\nThis option is not relevant to the argumentation, as it does not provide any information about the relationship between the skeletal fractures of prehistoric hominids and those of rodeo riders.\nIf there is no similarity between two effects, then there is probably no similarity between their causes. This principle undermines the argumentation by suggesting that without a similarity between the skeletal fractures of prehistoric hominids and those of rodeo riders, there is likely no similarity between their causes.. Therefore, my final option is: "]]
